Looking at this problem, I need to determine which conditions logically necessitate which others, for all possible partitions (h High-Priority, l Legacy, h+l=35, h,l≥1) and all distinct duration assignments.

**Setup.** Let H = High-Priority set (|H|=h), L = Legacy set (|L|=l). Define:
- **C1:** min(H) > max(L) (all H above all L)
- **C2:** A > B, where A = #{L logs < min(H)}, B = #{H logs < max(L)}
- **C3:** C > D, where C = #{H logs > min(L)}, D = #{L logs > min(H)}
- **C4:** mean(L) < mean(H)

**Key identities** (durations distinct, so no ties):
- A + D = l (every L log is either below or above min(H)), so A = l − D.
- #{H > max(L)} = h − B, and since min(L) ≤ max(L), we have C = #{H > min(L)} ≥ #{H > max(L)} = h − B.

---

**C1 ⟹ C2, C3, C4 (all three hold).**

If C1: min(H) > max(L), so every L log < min(H) giving A = l, and every H log > max(L) giving B = 0. C2: l > 0 ✓ (L non-empty). Similarly C = h, D = 0, so C3: h > 0 ✓ (H non-empty). Since every H duration > every L duration, mean(H) > mean(L), so C4 ✓.

**C2 ⟹̸ C1.** Counterexample (h=2, l=33): H at {40, 60}, L at {1,...,31, 50, 100}. Then A = 31, B = 2, C2: 31 > 2 ✓. But L has logs at 50, 100 > 40 = min(H), so C1 fails.

**C2 ⟹̸ C3.** Counterexample (h=1, l=34): H at {50}, L at {1,...,33, 100}. Then A = 33, B = 1 (H < max(L)=100), C2: 33 > 1 ✓. But C = 1 (H > min(L)=1), D = 1 (one L > min(H)=50), C3: 1 > 1 fails.

**C2 ⟹̸ C4.** Counterexample (h=1, l=34): H at {50}, L at {1,...,33, 10000}. Then A = 33, B = 1, C2 ✓. But mean(L) = (561+10000)/34 ≈ 310.6 > 50 = mean(H), so C4 fails.

**C3 ⟹̸ C1.** Counterexample (h=20, l=15): H at {1, 100,...,119}, L at {2,...,16}. Then C = 19 (H logs > min(L)=2), D = 15 (all L > min(H)=1), C3: 19 > 15 ✓. But H has a log at 1 < all L, so C1 fails.

**C3 ⟹̸ C2.** Same counterexample: A = l − D = 15 − 15 = 0, B = #{H < max(L)=16} = 1, C2: 0 > 1 fails.

**C3 ⟹̸ C4.** Counterexample (h=30, l=5): H at {1,...,10, 100,...,119}, L at {5, 6, 7, 8, 10000}. Then C = 25 (H > min(L)=5), D = 5 (all L > min(H)=1), C3: 25 > 5 ✓. But mean(L) = 10026/5 = 2005.2 ≫ mean(H) ≈ 74.8, so C4 fails.

**C4 ⟹̸ C1.** Counterexample (h=34, l=1): H at {1,...,33, 1000}, L at {30}. mean(H) ≈ 45.9 > 30 = mean(L), C4 ✓. But H has logs 1–29 < 30, so C1 fails.

**C4 ⟹̸ C2.** Same counterexample: A = #{L < min(H)=1} = 0, B = #{H < max(L)=30} = 29, C2: 0 > 29 fails.

**C4 ⟹̸ C3.** Counterexample (h=34, l=1): H at {1,...,33, 10000}, L at {50}. mean(H) ≈ 310.6 > 50, C4 ✓. But C = #{H > 50} = 1, D = #{L > 1} = 1, C3: 1 > 1 fails.

---

**Summary of implications** (a_{i,j} = 1):

| | j=1 | j=2 | j=3 | j=4 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| i=1 | — | **1** | **1** | **1** |
| i=2 | 0 | — | 0 | 0 |
| i=3 | 0 | 0 | — | 0 |
| i=4 | 0 | 0 | 0 | — |

The only implications are C1 → C2, C1 → C3, C1 → C4.

$$\sum_{i=1}^4 \sum_{j \in S, j \neq i} a_{i,j} = 1 + 1 + 1 = \boxed{3}$$

